  "description": "The classic active Baxandall tone control: Bass and Treble, each from about -20 dB cut through flat (half-way) to about +20 dB boost, with the mids untouched. Values and refs from Texas Instruments' application report SLOA042, \"Audio Tone Control Using The TLC074 Operational Amplifier\" (2000), Figure 3 (R11, R19, C9, C7, R14 around the bass pot R10A; C12, C11, R16 around the treble pot R18A; U2:D); a TL071 for the TLC074's quarter, on 9 V with + at vref instead of TI's 5 V and mid-supply. Linear pots (the report marks only its volume pots audio). Left out: TI's input buffer and volume, and its 100 Ω and 47k at the output; TI's 2.2 µF input cap C1 is kept, at the network's input. It needs a low-impedance source: inside an effect, an op-amp output or an emitter or source follower before it is enough; from the guitar, a pot or a transistor's collector, put a buffer first (Buffer · op-amp (Klon)). The output sits at vref. Wire vref to a Vref divider.",
  "tags": [
    "tone",
    "tone stack",
    "opamp",
    "baxandall"
  ],
